{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790445839,"nonce":"DohvLJ/FA2/+Vf1l7w8n7Z1TNak=","title":"Inference: a local read before its assignment is typed Object and pins what it flows into","message":"Typer::expr gives a Name that is in no scope yet Ty::Object. In the\nfirst walker pass of a body, a list literal decided at `xs = []` reads\nthe elements appended later in the loop before the loop variable is\nassigned, so xs is List(Object) for that pass, and a variable joined\nfrom it keeps Object for good:\n\n    def main():\n        xs = []\n        for i in range(4):\n            xs.append(i)\n        s = 0\n        for x in xs:\n            s += x\n        return s\n\ntypes xs: List(Int) but x: Object and s: Object. The same happens to\ndicts (a loop variable over a list of dict literals becomes\nDict(Str, Object)), and, since joined dict shapes share one store, it\nwidens the dicts' storage too. Reading a name the body assigns later as\nUnknown (seeding the body's bound names as Unknown before the rounds)\nwould let the later pass decide it.","files":null}]}